Alabama's Attorney General is suing the  mayor of Birmingham for covering up a  confederate memorial that 52 foot  monument honoring southern soldiers and  sailors is now surrounded by wooden  panels it is one of many Confederate  symbols across the country that could be  removed in the wake of the weekend  violence in Charlottesville some cities  are already taking away statues and  plaques Omar Villafranca is in  Birmingham at the monument the mayor is  targeting Omar good morning good morning  this monument was put up in 1905 by the  United Daughters of the Confederacy  there's also a new state law that says  monuments that have been around for 40  years cannot be removed or altered and  this wall is considered an alteration  Mayor William Bell says he plans to  fight the law because he wants this  monument pulled down we have no real  connection with the Civil War but we are  definitely a civil rights city in terms  that this was the cradle of the Civil  Rights Movement Mayor William Bell says  Birmingham was founded after the end of  the Civil War and the monument conflicts  with the city's deep history in the  struggle for civil rights you don't want  to see it no because again think about  what he represents the Confederacy was  an act of sedition it was an act of  treason Tuesday night he ordered city  workers to put up plywood walls around  the memorial that has stood here for  more than a century Birmingham is not  alone Baltimore's mayor had four  Confederate monuments removed without  warning before dawn Wednesday I did what  was right for my city that was my intent  and it is done other Confederate  monuments are under consideration for  removal  some have been vandalized or even  destroyed at least four people have been  arrested in connection to the toppling  of that Confederate statue in Durham  North Carolina Monday night  mayor Bell says the monument in  Birmingham represents something that  should have been put to rest a long time  ago you're a black mayor of a city that  was crucial in the civil rights movement  what do you want to happen to that  statue I would like to see us giving it  back to the Daughters of the Confederacy  and if they want to put it on private  property you know that's their business  we reached out to the United Daughters  of the Confederacy but did not hear back  mayor Bell says this statue does not  belong in the city that he says broke  the back of segregation in America  Charlie thanks Omar
 

